# What You're Doing, second half of the intro: the ostinato (figure 63.1)
# Pollack types the ostinato in eighth notes, eight to a bar, with "-" for each held eighth:
# D - - F# B A F# E | - - E D E F# - -. The accents (>) fall on the E at "four-and" (tied over
# the bar line) and the F# at "three-and" of the next bar; the last four notes echo "what you're
# doin'". Added: the ostinato's second statement in bars 3-4 (he describes a "dual-statement"
# but types it once).
#? The essay says the ostinato starts with a syncopation from the "four-and" of the previous bar; as typed, the first D is on the downbeat. Check whether that D is really an eighth early.
#? Octave (D4 to B4) chosen; the figure is on guitar and piano.
key D

system
voice "Ostinato"  | D4*1.5 F#4*0.5 B4*0.5 A4*0.5 F#4*0.5 E4*0.5 | -*1 E4*0.5 D4*0.5 E4*0.5 F#4*1.5 | D4*1.5 F#4*0.5 B4*0.5 A4*0.5 F#4*0.5 E4*0.5 | -*1 E4*0.5 D4*0.5 E4*0.5 F#4*1.5 |
words "Accents"   | r*3.5 >*0.5 | r*2.5 >*0.5 r*1 | r*3.5 >*0.5 | r*2.5 >*0.5 r*1 |
chords "Chords"   | D{I} | G{IV} | D{I} | G{IV} |
